---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { apps } from '../../data/site';
const app = apps.find((item) => item.slug === 'killmyapps')!;
---
<BaseLayout title="KillMyApps" description={app.description}>
  <div class="container page">
    <p><a class="back-link" href="/projects/">← Projects</a></p>
    <header class="page-header project-detail-header"><span class="eyebrow">Android Utility · {app.version}</span><h1>{app.name}</h1><p>{app.tagline}</p><div class="actions"><a class="button primary" href={app.download}>Download</a><a class="button" href={app.github}>View on GitHub</a></div></header>
    <section class="detail-intro"><div class="detail-stat"><strong>Android 8+</strong><span>Minimum version</span></div><div class="detail-stat"><strong>Root / Shizuku</strong><span>Execution layers</span></div><div class="detail-stat"><strong>GPL-3.0</strong><span>Open source</span></div></section>
    <section class="section prose"><span class="eyebrow">Overview</span><h2>A focused system utility.</h2><p>{app.description}</p><p>KillMyApps is designed around a simple idea: when you need to stop an Android app, the action should be immediate and predictable. It avoids becoming a general app manager and keeps the interface centered on termination.</p></section>
    <section class="section prose"><span class="eyebrow">The problem</span><h2>Background apps should be under your control.</h2><p>Android devices can accumulate applications that keep processes alive, restart in the background, or continue consuming idle resources. For power users, reaching the right system action through standard settings can be slower than the task itself.</p><p>KillMyApps provides a direct, repeatable path for terminating selected applications while keeping the scope intentionally narrow.</p></section>
    <section class="section"><span class="eyebrow">Approach</span><h2>Built around direct execution.</h2><div class="grid"><article class="card"><h3>Root & Shizuku</h3><p>Privileged execution through Root or Shizuku keeps the app focused on the actual system operation instead of workarounds.</p></article><article class="card"><h3>Source-aware selection</h3><p>User, Launchable, and System sources make selection flexible while helping users avoid accidental operations.</p></article><article class="card"><h3>Selection Mode</h3><p>Choose exactly which applications should be terminated rather than relying on a fixed or opaque list.</p></article><article class="card"><h3>Automation</h3><p>Auto Killer handles recurring background processes for users who want consistent resource control.</p></article><article class="card"><h3>Quick access</h3><p>Quick Settings Tile and home-screen widgets make the action available without opening a full workflow.</p></article><article class="card"><h3>Focused UI</h3><p>The interface stays lightweight and task-oriented instead of growing into a general-purpose app manager.</p></article></div></section>
    <section class="section prose"><span class="eyebrow">Architecture</span><h2>Keep the control path small.</h2><p>The application separates the user-facing selection and state from the privileged execution layer. This keeps Root and Shizuku integration behind a focused boundary while the UI can evolve independently.</p><p>That separation also matters for reliability: selection, filtering, automation, widgets, and Quick Settings entry points can share the same core behavior instead of implementing separate kill flows.</p></section>
    <section class="section prose"><span class="eyebrow">System integration</span><h2>Where Android meets the tool.</h2><p>The project sits close to Android's process-management layer and relies on privileged execution paths for operations that are intentionally outside the normal application sandbox.</p><p>Supporting both Root and Shizuku gives users two execution models while allowing the same product experience to remain simple and predictable.</p></section>
    <section class="section prose"><span class="eyebrow">Performance</span><h2>Fast by design.</h2><p>Performance is treated as part of the product rather than a later optimization pass. Filtering, selection, background work, and the privileged execution path are kept focused so the common one-tap workflow stays responsive.</p><p>The same principle drives the UI: useful information is visible when needed, while advanced controls remain out of the way until the user asks for them.</p></section>
    <section class="section"><span class="eyebrow">Release history</span><h2>How it evolved.</h2><div class="release-list"><div><strong>v1.5</strong><span>Auto Killer, 1×1 and 1×3 widgets, Shizuku UserService/AIDL work, Android 17 support, UI and performance improvements.</span></div><div><strong>v1.4</strong><span>Quick Settings Tile, Selection Mode, animation controls, stable Shizuku integration, filtering and performance work.</span></div><div><strong>v1.3</strong><span>Shizuku support and Killer Mode, expanding the execution and control model.</span></div><div><strong>v1.2</strong><span>Continued refinement of the core utility and the path toward the newer feature set.</span></div><div><strong>v1.1</strong><span>Early post-stable improvements and iteration on the core workflow.</span></div><div><strong>v1.0.0</strong><span>First stable release and the foundation of the focused app-killing workflow.</span></div></div></section>
    <section class="section prose"><span class="eyebrow">Design decisions</span><h2>Keep it small. Keep it fast.</h2><p>A recurring product decision is to resist features that turn KillMyApps into a full app manager. Profiles and preset systems, for example, add state across widgets, Quick Settings, shortcuts, and selection UI without improving the core action enough to justify that complexity.</p><p>The goal is a tool that users can open, trigger, and leave alone—while still offering enough system-level control for advanced Android setups.</p></section>
    <section class="section"><span class="eyebrow">What's next</span><h2>More control, without more clutter.</h2><p class="muted-copy">Upcoming work includes Kill Level, a redesigned animated Toast, revised animations, and a rewritten Auto Killer moving from experimental behavior toward a stable implementation.</p></section>
    <section class="section"><div class="actions"><a class="button primary" href={app.download}>Get KillMyApps</a><a class="button" href={app.github}>Explore the source</a></div></section>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
